# Guardrails

A shell hook (`.cursor/hooks/guard-shell.mjs`) enforces the most dangerous of these. Rules still apply where the hook cannot see.

Never:
- Change a database schema or data by hand (psql, mongosh, redis-cli writes). Schema changes go through migrations; data changes go through app code or seed scripts.
- Edit or delete an applied migration.
- Run destructive commands: `docker compose down -v`, `docker volume rm/prune`, `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`, `git clean -f`, `git branch -D`, dropping databases/tables, `FLUSHALL`.
- Read, print, or edit `.env` or any secret; use `.env.example` instead.
- Weaken, skip, or delete tests to make them pass.

Ask the user first:
- Running E2E tests (`pnpm test:e2e`, `playwright test`): they build and start all apps on ports 3000/4173.
- `git push`, opening PRs, or anything that leaves this machine.
- Opening a database client (psql, mongosh, redis-cli), even read-only.
- Installing global tools, starting/stopping Docker or system services, killing processes the agent didn't start.
- Adding a dependency, or changing CI, Docker, or tooling config outside the task card.
